%T A401163 499,503,509,521,563,599,607,613,617,643,647,653,659,661,719,727,733,
%U A401163 769,821,829,839,853,857,859,881,883,919,947,953,967,977,983,997,1021,1049,1051
%N A401163 Primes p such that, considering only splits containing at least one composite segment, the set of terminal primes reachable along shortest paths has cardinality at least 2.
%C A401163 The process: Given a prime p, consider all ways to split its decimal representation into consecutive segments such that (i) no segment equals 1, (ii) no segment has a leading zero, and (iii) at least one segment is composite. For each such split, replace every composite segment by its prime factors in ascending order, then concatenate all resulting primes, preserving the original order of segments. If the concatenated number is prime, the process terminates; otherwise, repeat the splitting on the new number. A terminal prime is a prime at which the process terminates. It is conjectured that every prime eventually terminates.
%C A401163 This sequence lists primes p for which the set of terminal primes reachable along paths of minimum length has cardinality at least 2. Primes with no legal split containing a composite segment are not in this sequence.
%H A401163 Sean A. Irvine, <a href="/A401163/b401163.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a> (terms 1..827 from Li Jingtao)
%H A401163 Li Jingtao, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23393923">Prime reincarnation</a>, Zenodo preprint, 2026.
%H A401163 Li Jingtao, <a href="/A401163/a401163.txt">Python program</a>.
%e A401163 From p = 89, at least two shortest paths (all of length 2) lead to distinct terminal primes:
%e A401163 Path 1: 89 -> split [8,9] -> factorize 8=2^3, 9=3^2 -> concatenate -> 22233
%e A401163 Then 22233 -> split [2,2,2,33] -> factorize 33=3*11 -> concatenate -> 222311 (prime, terminal)
%e A401163 Path 2: 89 -> split [8,9] -> factorize 8=2^3, 9=3^2 -> concatenate -> 22233
%e A401163 Then 22233 -> split [2,2233] -> factorize 2233=7*11*29 -> concatenate -> 271129 (prime, terminal)
%e A401163 Path 3: 89 -> split [8,9] -> factorize 8=2^3, 9=3^2 -> concatenate -> 22233
%e A401163 Then 22233 -> split [222,33] -> factorize 222=2*3*37, 33=3*11 -> concatenate -> 2337311 (prime, terminal)
%e A401163 Since the set of terminal primes {222311, 271129, 2337311} has cardinality >= 2, 89 is in the sequence.
